WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:07.760
Why Every Nonprofit Should Support a one percent treaty Nonprofits fighting disease currently compete for the same shrinking

2
00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:14.560
pile of donor money. A one percent treaty creates twenty-seven point two billion dollars in new funding.

3
00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:21.620
Here's how to get every charitable organization to stop fighting over crumbs and start fighting for the bakery.

4
00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:29.780
Your nonprofit sector is a bit like thousands of starving dogs fighting over a single bowl of food while an entire banquet

5
00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:34.300
sits untouched behind them. The dogs are very focused on the bowl.

6
00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:43.350
They've developed sophisticated bowl-fighting strategies. Some dogs have hired consultants to optimize their bowl access.

7
00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:48.360
None of them have turned around. I have watched this for eighty years.

8
00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:57.380
Malaria kills six hundred thousand people a year and has a seven billion dollar nonprofit ecosystem dedicated to it.

9
00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:06.320
Polio has been "nearly eradicated" for twenty-five years. Cancer charities have raised hundreds of billions of dollars and

10
00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:14.880
cancer is still the second leading cause of death. Occasionally, as a side effect of all this activity, a problem gets slightly

11
00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:23.590
less bad. This is celebrated with an awards dinner. Every grant you win is a grant the Alzheimer's people lose.

12
00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:28.200
Every dollar you secure for malaria is a dollar that doesn't go to cancer.

13
00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:35.860
Every donor you convince is a donor distracted from the next organization, which is also trying to stop people from dying,

14
00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:43.740
which is the same thing you're trying to do, but you're competing because your species decided that "saving lives" should

15
00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:51.200
be a competitive market with limited funding. On Wishonia, this would be classified as a psychological disorder.

16
00:01:51.920 --> 00:02:00.020
On Earth, it's called "the nonprofit sector.". Meanwhile, the reason the bowl is so small sits right there in your budget

17
00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:10.160
documents, fully visible, discussed by no one: Your species spends six hundred and four times more on weapons than on clinical

18
00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:19.860
trials to discover which medicines actually work. Military spending: two point seven two trillion dollars per year and rising.

19
00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:28.140
Medical research: sixty-seven point five billion dollars (of which only four point five billion dollars funds government

20
00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:34.760
clinical trials) and facing real-term cuts. The bowl for solving disease is shrinking.

21
00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:43.800
The bowl for building weapons is expanding. You are rearranging deck chairs on the Titanic, except the deck chairs are grants

22
00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:50.020
and the iceberg is two point seven two trillion dollars pointed at everyone's face.

23
00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:57.700
This is not an ideological claim. It's arithmetic. The kind where you multiply numbers together and get sad.

24
00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:06.860
The logic behind a one percent treaty is the logic behind turning around and noticing the banquet: stop optimizing inside

25
00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:15.080
the smallest slice of the pie. Make the pie bigger. Turn a zero-sum game into a positive-sum game.

26
00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:20.560
This requires no moral evolution. It requires looking behind you.

27
00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:29.520
Why the Banquet Exists and Nobody Has Noticed. On Wishonia, when we noticed that our species was spending the vast majority

28
00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:34.840
of its resources on activities that reduced the number of living citizens, we stopped.

29
00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:39.220
It took about a week. In fairness, we didn't have consultants.

30
00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:48.840
On Earth, every organization fighting a problem is fighting a symptom of the same upstream cause: your species allocates

31
00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:54.460
resources toward destruction and then wonders why everything is being destroyed.

32
00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:59.800
War is upstream of poverty (hard to build an economy when someone keeps blowing it up).

33
00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:07.720
War is upstream of pollution (militaries are among the largest polluters on Earth, which is ironic given they're supposedly

34
00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:15.720
protecting it). War is upstream of refugee crises (humans whose homes were converted into craters need somewhere else to

35
00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:22.380
live). War is upstream of disease spread (bombs do not improve public health infrastructure).

36
00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:30.040
War is upstream of technological stagnation (yes, DARPA invented the internet; military R.

37
00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:37.300
and D. occasionally produces breakthroughs as a side effect of spectacular waste, the same way a forest fire occasionally

38
00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:41.400
clears space for new growth, but nobody calls an arsonist a gardener).

39
00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:50.640
War is the root password to your worst problems. Redirect one percent of it, and every downstream mission gets easier.

40
00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:54.820
Increase it by one percent, and every mission becomes nearly impossible.

41
00:04:55.760 --> 00:05:01.080
This is not a peace movement. Nobody has to hold hands or sing songs or learn each other's names.

42
00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:07.700
They just have to do arithmetic, which is the one thing your species is actually good at when it bothers to try.

43
00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:14.980
I want to explain why this applies to every type of organization, but I'm going to do it by describing what I observe rather

44
00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:22.140
than what you should do, because your species responds poorly to instructions and reasonably well to feeling embarrassed.

45
00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:28.850
Environmental groups spend their time trying to reduce carbon emissions while the largest polluters on Earth (militaries)

46
00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:36.980
operate completely outside every climate agreement. Defunding them one percent would be the most effective climate policy

47
00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:42.620
ever proposed, and it doesn't require anyone to give up hamburgers or install a solar panel.

48
00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:49.880
Nobody has suggested this because the environmental movement and the peace movement operate in separate buildings and do

49
00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:54.300
not talk to each other. They attend different conferences. They have different donors.

50
00:05:54.680 --> 00:06:01.260
They fight the same problem from different angles and never coordinate, which is exactly the behavior they're trying to

51
00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:09.640
fix in the systems they criticize. Anti-poverty groups have been running the strategy of "ask rich people to feel bad" for

52
00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:15.960
sixty years. How's that going? War and disease are the root causes of most modern poverty.

53
00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:24.800
A one percent treaty tackles both. But poverty organizations don't work on military spending because that's "not their issue."

54
00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:28.140
Their issue is poverty. Which is caused by military spending.

55
00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:35.140
Which is not their issue. Peace organizations have been making moral appeals for seventy years.

56
00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:44.120
Moral appeals do not work on a species that runs on money. A one percent treaty makes peace more profitable than war.

57
00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:51.740
This is the only argument that has ever worked on humans, and the peace movement refuses to make it because it feels unseemly

58
00:06:51.740 --> 00:06:58.400
to talk about profit when people are dying. So people keep dying, but at least the peace movement's messaging is ethically

59
00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:04.500
pure. Disease advocacy groups are fighting other disease groups for crumbs of the N.I.H.

60
00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:10.300
budget. You are literally competing with cancer patients for the money that should be curing cancer patients.

61
00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:18.580
A one percent treaty creates twenty-seven point two billion dollars in new funding with twelve point three times more trial

62
00:07:18.580 --> 00:07:25.900
capacity. The bottleneck isn't ideas or molecules. It's running enough trials to find out what works.

63
00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:32.880
This solves that. If your organization is a five zero one c three, you're already thinking "we can't lobby."

64
00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:40.000
You can, actually, within limits (the "no substantial part" test, or the five zero one h election caps).

65
00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:45.500
But more importantly, supporting a referendum is educational activity, not lobbying.

66
00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:53.420
And for the heavy political lifting, the legal architecture uses a five zero one c four that faces no lobbying limits at

67
00:07:53.420 --> 00:07:56.800
all. Your lawyers will feel better after reading that chapter.

68
00:07:57.220 --> 00:08:03.100
But Shouldn't Nonprofits Focus on Their Core Mission? Yes. That is exactly the point.

69
00:08:03.160 --> 00:08:09.620
Your core mission is failing because your resources are shrinking while the resources of the people making your mission

70
00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:17.140
harder are expanding. This is like a firefighter insisting they should "focus on firefighting" while someone is actively

71
00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:21.060
pouring gasoline on the fire and nobody is addressing the gasoline.

72
00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:27.680
Every nonprofit today operates inside a system that guarantees their failure.

73
00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:30.780
Too few grant dollars. Too much competition for those dollars.

74
00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:34.760
Shrinking government budgets for health, climate, and poverty.

75
00:08:34.840 --> 00:08:42.159
Ballooning budgets for war. Donor fatigue (your donors are tired because you keep asking them for money to do things that

76
00:08:42.159 --> 00:08:50.600
don't work because you don't have enough money). And the people whose job is "convince politicians that killing is profitable"

77
00:08:50.660 --> 00:08:55.600
get two point seven two trillion dollars annually to make your job harder.

78
00:08:56.320 --> 00:09:03.020
Your future funding and impact will be determined by either (a) you, or (b) Lockheed Martin's lobbying department.

79
00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:09.300
Currently it's (b). You seem fine with this. The Obvious Solution That Nobody Sees.

80
00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:14.300
Right now, every grant you win is a grant another organization loses.

81
00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:20.960
This is called a "zero-sum game," which your species invented the mathematics to describe and then immediately forgot to

82
00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:26.720
apply to anything useful. A one percent treaty turns this into a positive-sum game.

83
00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:31.220
twenty-seven point two billion dollars per year in new funding.

84
00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:38.340
Malaria gets cured AND Alzheimer's gets cured. You stop writing proposals and start getting paid for results.

85
00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:45.360
This is like discovering that instead of fighting over one pizza, you can just order more pizzas than anyone can eat.

86
00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:52.040
Except the pizzas are billions of dollars and the ordering is international treaty ratification, which admittedly takes

87
00:09:52.040 --> 00:09:58.080
longer than calling Domino's. Countries still keep ninety-nine percent of their apocalypse capacity.

88
00:09:58.420 --> 00:10:02.340
They can still end all life nineteen times instead of twenty.

89
00:10:02.840 --> 00:10:08.420
If you can't successfully end the world with nineteen attempts, the twentieth probably wasn't going to help.

90
00:10:09.140 --> 00:10:16.380
But that single percentage point creates a twenty-seven point two billion dollars a year one percent Treaty Fund, funds

91
00:10:16.380 --> 00:10:22.560
global decentralized clinical trials, and makes every nonprofit's mission dramatically more feasible.

92
00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:27.240
Move one percent from the mountain to the puddle. The puddle becomes a lake.

93
00:10:27.620 --> 00:10:34.360
The mountain is still a mountain. Everybody wins except the people who were selling mountain insurance, and even they can

94
00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:38.740
pivot. Why Your Species Only Changes When the Building Is On Fire.

95
00:10:39.860 --> 00:10:47.040
Your species only reorganizes resources when things are going badly enough that ignoring the problem becomes more painful

96
00:10:47.060 --> 00:10:54.400
than fixing it. This is not a theory. It is the entirety of your political history, from the French Revolution to the New

97
00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:58.420
Deal to the creation of NATO. Comfortable humans don't change things.

98
00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:03.860
Uncomfortable humans change everything. Then they get comfortable and stop changing things again.

99
00:11:05.180 --> 00:11:10.770
Right now, for the first time in eighty years, everything is going wrong at the same time.

100
00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:14.360
Military budgets are expanding while everything else gets cut.

101
00:11:15.100 --> 00:11:19.100
Trust in government spending priorities has reached "used car salesman" levels.

102
00:11:19.780 --> 00:11:27.560
Pandemics exposed the cost of underfunding resilience. Climate disasters have shifted public opinion from "debatable" to

103
00:11:27.560 --> 00:11:35.540
"my house is underwater." And your leaders are searching for a unifying narrative, which is political language for "someone

104
00:11:35.540 --> 00:11:40.000
please tell us what to do before we get voted out.". This is your window.

105
00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:43.660
It opens when humans are scared and closes when they get distracted.

106
00:11:44.380 --> 00:11:51.140
Based on my four thousand two hundred and ninety-seven years of observation, the average window lasts about eighteen months

107
00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:55.980
before your species discovers a new television show and forgets the crisis entirely.

108
00:11:56.960 --> 00:12:02.570
The Factory Precedent. During World War II, your species did something remarkable.

109
00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:11.540
G.M. built tanks. Ford built B-twenty-four bombers. Frigidaire (yes, the refrigerator people) produced machine guns.

110
00:12:12.140 --> 00:12:16.600
Typewriter manufacturers made rifles. Not because they "supported war."

111
00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:20.520
Because the system needed realignment and ignoring it was impossible.

112
00:12:21.060 --> 00:12:26.950
Also because the government asked very firmly, in a tone that strongly implied "this isn't really a question.".

113
00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:34.860
Your nonprofit sector is the modern equivalent of those car factories: massive capacity, talented people, pointed at the

114
00:12:34.860 --> 00:12:40.480
wrong problem. You have millions of humans optimizing spreadsheets for the wrong budget.

115
00:12:41.140 --> 00:12:48.420
The factories pivoted in months. Your nonprofits have been "focused on their core mission" for decades while the core mission

116
00:12:48.420 --> 00:12:56.460
gets harder every year. You didn't get better at focus. You got better at ignoring the context in which your focus operates.

117
00:12:57.240 --> 00:13:04.300
A few years of effort to shift one percent of global budgets could produce more impact than thirty years of incremental

118
00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:09.670
programs. Decades during which, I should note, the problems got worse.

119
00:13:09.940 --> 00:13:15.460
On Wishonia, we have a word for "doing the same thing for thirty years while results decline."

120
00:13:16.060 --> 00:13:20.840
The word is "hobby.". "Why Not Just Run Trials Without the Treaty?".

121
00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:25.820
You should. But then what? You've built a beautiful garden hose.

122
00:13:25.840 --> 00:13:32.420
You've optimized its diameter, its nozzle, its flow rate. You've published papers about its efficiency.

123
00:13:32.820 --> 00:13:39.740
It is the best garden hose your species has ever produced. It's attached to a pipe with no water.

124
00:13:40.640 --> 00:13:48.000
Your clinical trial infrastructure becomes twelve point three times more useful when the resource stream feeding it is ten

125
00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:55.180
times larger. You cannot out-engineer a funding problem. Build the hose AND turn on the water.

126
00:13:55.600 --> 00:14:01.240
They're complements, not substitutes. What one percent Less Violence Buys Every Mission.

127
00:14:02.480 --> 00:14:08.620
I'm going to describe what happens when you shift one percent of military budgets, but I'm going to describe it the way

128
00:14:08.620 --> 00:14:16.480
I actually observe it, which is as a single upstream change that fixes things your species has been trying to fix separately

129
00:14:16.480 --> 00:14:21.160
for decades while refusing to notice they're all the same problem.

130
00:14:22.100 --> 00:14:29.780
Twelve point three times more clinical trial capacity. Diseases get cured instead of managed (the pharmaceutical industry's

131
00:14:29.860 --> 00:14:37.560
least favorite sentence). Militaries emit less pollution (nobody carbon-taxes a tank, but maybe they should).

132
00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:43.500
Fewer resource-driven conflicts means fewer refugees means less poverty.

133
00:14:44.280 --> 00:14:49.880
Everyone becomes slightly less dead, which is the lowest bar imaginable, and yet.

134
00:14:50.820 --> 00:14:57.960
And here is the part that should keep every nonprofit Executive Director awake at night: you stop writing grant proposals

135
00:14:57.960 --> 00:15:02.400
and start actually doing your mission. Remember your mission?

136
00:15:02.760 --> 00:15:05.620
It was in the brochure. The one you wrote to get the grant.

137
00:15:06.060 --> 00:15:10.900
To pay the person who writes the grants. To fund the office where the grant-writing happens.

138
00:15:11.280 --> 00:15:18.980
Your organization has become a machine whose primary output is requests for money and whose secondary output is occasionally,

139
00:15:20.680 --> 00:15:28.700
accidentally, the thing you set out to do. The treaty fixes this by making the money show up without the begging.

140
00:15:29.580 --> 00:15:37.780
Your species calls this "too good to be true," which is a phrase you use to describe things that are good and true but embarrassing

141
00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:48.100
to admit. On Bribing Nonprofits (Legally). On Wishonia, when we wanted organizations to cooperate on a shared goal, we explained

142
00:15:48.100 --> 00:15:52.140
the goal and they cooperated. On Earth, you need to pay them.

143
00:15:52.760 --> 00:15:59.960
This is not a criticism. It's a design parameter. I've learned to work with the species I have, not the species I wish I

144
00:15:59.960 --> 00:16:07.740
had. Asking nonprofits to support a one percent treaty out of moral obligation is like asking a drowning person to rescue

145
00:16:07.760 --> 00:16:12.720
other swimmers. They're drowning. They need a life preserver, not a guilt trip.

146
00:16:13.600 --> 00:16:19.800
The life preserver: you make the organization rich. Old way: "Here's one hundred thousand dollars.

147
00:16:20.060 --> 00:16:24.780
Hope it helps. Submit a forty-seven page report in six months about impacts and learnings.

148
00:16:25.400 --> 00:16:32.760
Use the word 'stakeholder' at least fourteen times." New way: "Here's fifty thousand dollars baseline to get started.

149
00:16:33.300 --> 00:16:40.060
Plus ten thousand dollars for every ten thousand verified referendum votes you drive through your unique link.

150
00:16:40.180 --> 00:16:48.860
No cap." The nonprofit's most rational path to maximizing their budget becomes: be your most effective mobilization partner.

151
00:16:49.780 --> 00:16:58.620
One metric. No ambiguity. Get paid for results. This is how every functional industry works except charity, which explains

152
00:16:58.620 --> 00:17:02.760
why every problem charity was invented to solve is still here.

153
00:17:03.560 --> 00:17:12.920
And then the ocean itself: the one percent Treaty Fund receives twenty-seven point two billion dollars annually.

154
00:17:12.980 --> 00:17:20.819
Your decentralized institutes of health allocates multi-billion dollar budgets to the diseases your organization exists

155
00:17:20.819 --> 00:17:28.980
to fight. When the treaty passes, the grant-writing stops. The money arrives because the treaty says it arrives.

156
00:17:29.700 --> 00:17:33.200
You don't need influence. You don't need a seat at the table.

157
00:17:33.840 --> 00:17:40.640
You need the treaty to pass. Everything after that is automatic, the way gravity is automatic, except instead of pulling

158
00:17:40.640 --> 00:17:46.460
things down it pulls money toward curing diseases. (Gravity has never done this.

159
00:17:46.520 --> 00:17:55.780
I checked.). You have two options. Option A: keep competing for finite grants forever, writing proposals, begging foundations,

160
00:17:55.860 --> 00:18:04.500
hoping your competitor doesn't write a better proposal. Option B: help pass the treaty, get paid referral bonuses for every

161
00:18:04.500 --> 00:18:13.180
